  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 243 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nUpper level ridging is expected to set up across the region most of \nthis week, which will largely support a dry forecast. However, a \nshortwave is expected to track across the Dakotas Tuesday afternoon \ninto Wednesday night that may provide a slight cool off for portions \nof the region this week. Most of the forecast region is expected to \nsee above average temperatures, with highs in the mid 60s. However, \nfollowing the shortwave, cool air advection aloft is expected across \neastern Nebraska, which may spill over into northeast portions of \nthe forecast region. Even with this push of cooler air aloft, highs \nacross north central Nebraska remain near seasonal, with highs in \nthe lower to mid 50s expected through Thursday. Something else to \nkeep an eye on will be the increasing winds Tuesday and Wednesday as \na surface low tracks across South Dakota. Southwesterly to westerly \nwinds are expected on Tuesday, with gusts up to 30 mph. By \nWednesday, northwest winds with gusts up to 35 mph are possible. \nWith humidity currently expected to drop as low as 25 to 30 percent \nboth days, will need to closely monitor for potential elevated fire \nweather concerns. Though a surface low is expected to track across \nSouth Dakota, still not expecting any precipitation across the \nforecast region. \n\nBy late week, upper level ridging becomes fully established across \nthe region, continuing to limit precipitation potential. As this \nbuilds in, warmer temperatures aloft track in from the west, which \nshould push the region back to above seasonal temperatures. Will \nstill keep an eye on the western extent of the cooler air, as some \nguidance keeps this over the very northeastern portions of the CWA. \nHowever, for the most part, highs are expected to rise back into the \n60s across the entire region, with mid 60s remaining across the \nwestern portion of the region. However, some of the higher end \nguidance does suggest the potential for some locations to possibly \nbreak into the lower 70s. Either way, this remains well above \naverage for mid November, when typical highs drop into the lower \n50s.",
